50,3,1,talk,Servas,,"The candidates are A: Microbiome Atlas, B: Protein Folding Dynamics, C: Regeneration in Amphibians, and D: Plant Drought Signaling.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,3,1,talk,Sakura,,A is definitely the winner! It's the most practical given the costs and the space.,"{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,3,1,talk,Daisuke,,I vote for A. It's the safest bet for a lasting contribution.,"{""words"":12,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,3,1,talk,Takumi,,A it is. No way I'm voting for something that relies on a broken spectrometer or a flooded field.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,3,1,talk,Minako,,I agree. A is the only one that doesn't clash with current facility bottlenecks.,"{""words"":14,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,3,2,talk,Servas,,"I concur. The staffing for modelling is strong, which supports the atlas's data needs.","{""words"":14,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
